@startuml
title Card definition vs. card instance

skinparam linetype ortho

/'
  A deck holds duplicates — two copies of Pikachu are the same DEFINITION but
  must take damage independently. So the definition (ICard, see ICard.puml) is
  immutable and shared, and every physical copy in a game is a CardInstance.
'/

class CardInstance {
    /' Identity within one Battle. Two copies of the same card are different
       instances with the same definition. '/
    - instanceId: int
    - definition: ICard
    - owner: Side
    - zone: Zone
}

enum Zone {
    DECK
    HAND
    ACTIVE
    BENCH
    DISCARD
    /' A Tool is in play but attached to a Pokemon rather than occupying a slot. '/
    ATTACHED
}

/'
  A CardInstance that is a Pokemon on the board. All the mutable battle state
  that used to sit on IPlayableCard lives here.
'/
class PokemonInPlay extends CardInstance {
    - damage: int
    - attachedEnergy: Map<Type, int>
    /' A Set, not an Optional: the real rules allow Poisoned and Burned to
       coexist with exactly ONE of Asleep / Paralyzed / Confused.
       See IStatus.puml. '/
    - statuses: Set<IStatus>
    - tool: Optional<CardInstance>
    /' Evolution and "played this turn" checks both need this. '/
    - turnPlayed: int
    /' Stage 1 played onto a Basic keeps the Basic underneath it. '/
    - evolutionStack: List<PokemonCard>
    /' Where IDurationEffect actually stores its result. See Damage.puml. '/
    - modifiers: List<ActiveModifier>

    + definition(): PokemonCard
    + maxHp(): int
    + currentHp(): int
    + isKnockedOut(): boolean
    + totalEnergy(): int
    + hasStatus(IStatus): boolean
    + addStatus(IStatus): void
    /' Statuses and duration modifiers are cleared when a Pokemon leaves the
       active spot or evolves. '/
    + clearStatuses(): void
}

CardInstance o-- ICard : definition
CardInstance o-- Zone
PokemonInPlay o-- IStatus
PokemonInPlay o-- Type : attached energy
PokemonInPlay *-- ActiveModifier

note right of CardInstance
  ITarget.resolve returns PokemonInPlay, and the card-level
  ICondition leaves are typed on CardInstance / PokemonInPlay
  rather than on the definition — `IsActive`, `IsBenched` and
  `InHand` are facts about a copy on the board, not about
  the card as printed.

  Because the instance knows its own `zone` and `owner`, those
  conditions need no ResolutionContext, which is why
  ICondition keeps its simple `evaluate(T)` signature.
end note

@enduml
